<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iskandar Muda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1583–<a href="/years/1636">1636</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iskandar Muda was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> from 1607 to 1636, the height of its power. He controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> trade of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, curbed the great merchant-nobles and conquered Aru, Pahang, Kedah and other states on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, carrying off their rulers and people. His great fleet failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> in 1629. His court was a centre of Malay Islamic learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda.jpg" alt="Iskandar Muda" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lukisan_Iskandar_muda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Iskandar Muda, whose name means young Alexander, was born about 1583, the son of Mansur Syah, a grandson of Sultan Abdul Jalil, and of Puteri Raja Inderabangsa, a daughter of the tenth sultan. He thus joined the two branches of the Acehnese dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-aceh">Hikayat Aceh</a>, which extols his qualities, describes his youth at length.</p><p>Known first as Perkasa Alam, he fell out about 1605 with his uncle Sultan Ali Ri'ayat Syah III and was imprisoned after a failed plan to rebel. When the Portuguese invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in 1606 he was released and distinguished himself in beating them back. Sultan Ali died suddenly on 4 April 1607, and Perkasa Alam secured the throne the same day, later having his other uncle Husain killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His power rested on a navy of heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> each carrying 600 to 800 men, a cavalry using Persian horses, an elephant corps, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> infantry and more than 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and guns. He took Deli in 1612, and in 1613 Aru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, bringing the sultan of Johor and his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, though Johor expelled the garrison later that year.</p><p>He defeated a Portuguese fleet at Bintan in 1614, conquered Pahang in 1617, Kedah in 1619, where the capital was laid waste, and Perak in 1620, and took Nias in 1624/5. In 1629 he sent several hundred ships against Portuguese Melaka; according to Portuguese sources, all were destroyed with 19,000 men. His sultanate kept northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> but never gained supremacy in the strait.</p><p>The economy rested on the spice trade, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, and he made the court the sole contact with foreign merchants. He suppressed the Acehnese elite, the orang kaya, by forcing them to attend court and barring them from building independent houses, and set up war leaders, hulubalang, and royal officials, panglima, in their place. He created courts using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>, and a palace guard of 3,000 women. His rule was brutal: a French visitor of the 1620s reported mutilations almost daily, and he had his own son Meurah Pupok executed, naming his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-thani">Iskandar Thani</a> heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Iskandar Muda died on 27 December 1636. He was the last sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> to descend in the male line from the founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mughayat-syah">Ali Mughayat Syah</a>. Under him Aceh became a centre of Islamic learning, drawing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> writers Hamzah Pansuri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syamsuddin-of-pasai">Syamsuddin of Pasai</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-aceh">Hikayat Aceh</a> probably dates from his reign. Among the Acehnese he is revered as a symbol of Aceh's past greatness, with the posthumous title Po Teuh Meureuhom, Our Beloved Late Lord.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iskandar Muda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Four queens ruled Aceh in succession from 1641 to 1699, beginning with Taj ul-Alam Safiatuddin Syah, daughter of the conqueror <b>Iskandar Muda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Aceh attacked repeatedly and finally conquered it in 1613 under <b>Iskandar Muda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/aru-kingdom">Aru Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that Asahan was founded around 1630 by Abdul Jalil, a son of Sultan <b>Iskandar Muda</b> of the Aceh Sultanate by a local woman, at the mouth of the Asahan river.</blockquote><a href="/state/asahan-sultanate">Asahan Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Deli was founded in 1632 by Tuanku Gocah Pahlawan, a commander sent by the Aceh Sultanate under <b>Iskandar Muda</b> to secure the coast of eastern Sumatra.</blockquote><a href="/state/deli-sultanate">Deli Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1617 <b>Iskandar Muda</b> of the Aceh Sultanate sacked Pahang and carried off its royal family.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-pahang-sultanate">Old Pahang Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Banda Aceh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=5.55,95.32&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Banda Aceh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Iskandar Muda"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5452 4526h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5452 4526h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=5.55,95.32&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
